{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["The reason for <b>And these are the names</b> is that Scripture intends to recount the matter of the exile from the time of their descent to Egypt, for then they went into exile at the head of the exiles, as I have explained. Therefore it turns back to the beginning of the matter, which is from the verse \"and all his seed he brought with him to Egypt\" (Genesis 46:7), and there it is written after it \"And these are the names of the children of Israel who came to Egypt,\" etc., and that very verse is what it repeated here. For even though they are two books, the narrative is connected by phrases that follow one another. When it mentioned the sons of Jacob it was brief regarding his grandsons and all his seed, and it summarized the totality as it said there (Genesis 46:27), \"All the souls of the house of Jacob who came to Egypt were seventy.\" <b>And like this matter</b> is in the book of Chronicles and the book of Ezra, for Chronicles concluded (2 Chronicles 36:22–23), \"And in the first year of Cyrus king of Persia, at the completion of the word of HaShem by the mouth of Jeremiah, HaShem stirred up the spirit of Cyrus,\" etc., \"Thus says Cyrus king of Persia,\" etc., and those two verses in their very language it repeated at the beginning of the book of Ezra (Ezra 1:1–2) to connect the narrative. Only, since they were two books, the first concluded with what preceded the building of the Temple and the second from the time of the building. And so too is the matter with these two books, Genesis and Exodus." "<b>And Rabbi</b> Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 1:1) that because it mentioned at the end of the first book that Joseph saw for his sons the third generation, it mentioned that his brothers too, when they descended, were few and were fruitful and multiplied. This is not correct. Rashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 1:1), \"Although He counted them in their lifetime, He returned and counted them after their death by their names, to make known His affection for them, that they are likened to stars, which He brings out by number and brings in by number, as it is said, 'Who brings out their host by number, to all of them He calls by name' (Isaiah 40:26).\" These are words of aggadah (Exodus Rabbah 1:3), and they are words of truth regarding the matter of affection — that HaShem cherishes them and repeats their names constantly — but the connection of the verses and their joining with the letter vav is as I have explained."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:01:29.508805845Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}